<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-II_lt" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Laws of Thermodynamics</title>

  <introduction>
    <p> 
   <alert> Heat and Work:</alert>
     When a body undergoes a displacement <m>\,ds</m> 
     by the action of a force <m>F</m>, the work done is <m>dW = F \cdot ds</m>. 
     When a body undergoes a change in temperature <m>\,dT</m> by the action of a heat source, 
     the heat absorbed is <m>dQ = C \cdot dT</m>, where <m>C</m> is the heat capacity of the 
     body. Both work and heat are forms of energy transfer, but they are not properties 
     of the system; rather, they are processes that occur during energy transfer. 
     Joul's experiment demonstrated that a certain amount of work can be converted into an equivalent amount of heat, 
     no matter what the process is. This led to the principle of conservation of energy. 
     </p>
    
  </introduction>

